# Replication Package — Switching Policy Specification

Companion artifacts for the paper *"Cross-domain Specification of Switching Policies
in Multi-level Simulation"* (submitted to the Journal of Systems and Software).

Archived version: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23804087

## Quick start

```
python3 tools/run_evaluation.py
```

That is the entire setup. Requirements: **Python >= 3.9, standard library only** —
no packages to install, no environment variables, no paths to configure (all paths
are resolved relative to the scripts). The command reproduces the tool-supported
part of the paper's evaluation (Section *Evaluation*) and exits non-zero if any
documented expectation is not met, so it doubles as a self-test of the package.

It runs seven studies and writes reports to `results/`. Studies 1--5 are the
mandatory, stdlib-only part; Studies 6--7 are the optional third-party
bindings (skipped without failing the suite if SUMO/Mesa are not installed).
They realize the paper's four evaluation methods as follows: method 1
(expressiveness/coverage mapping) is analytical and lives in the paper plus
`coding/coverage.csv`; method 2 (comparative expressiveness) = Study 3;
method 3 (tool-supported analyzability) = Studies 1--2 and 5; method 4
(executable instantiations) = Study 4 (E-site + traffic) and the optional
Studies 6 (SUMO) and 7 (Mesa):

1. **Well-formedness validation** (paper: Table "Tool-supported well-formedness
   validation"): validates three well-formed example policies and six policies
   with seeded violations against the constraints WF1, WF2, WF3a, WF3b, WF4,
   WF5. Expected outcome: all valid instances pass; every seeded violation is
   detected with exactly the expected constraint.
2. **Policy-set analysis** (paper: paragraph "Static conflict, oscillation,
   and rotation analysis"): analyzes a constructed conflicting set
   (`ping-pong-conflict`) and its repaired counterpart (`hysteresis-safe`),
   plus the n-policy rotation sets: `three-cycle-conflict` is well-formed in
   isolation (limits and priorities present, pairwise analyses silent) and
   must be flagged ONLY by the rotation analysis A3; `three-cycle-safe`
   (pairwise-disjoint activation bands) must be clean.
3. **Comparative expressiveness** (paper: Table "Comparative expressiveness"):
   mechanically derives, from the machine-readable coding matrices, whether
   each analyzed case is fully/partially/not expressible in ECA rules, Stitch,
   the FCC framework, and the proposed metamodel. Additionally reports the
   verdicts split into third-party sources vs. author-constructed scenarios
   and runs a **one-cell sensitivity analysis** (every capability cell moved
   one level up/down, all verdicts re-derived; `results/comparative_sensitivity.md`).
4. **Executable instantiations** (paper: Section "Executable Instantiation"):
   (a) *E-site*: the reference adaptation manager interprets the E-site policy
   set and drives switching in a two-level simulation. Expected outcome:
   policy-driven switching is cheaper than the always-detailed baseline and more
   accurate than the always-coarse baseline; the decision-layer overhead is
   measured (median over repeated runs, measurement environment recorded in
   `results/scenario_metrics.json`); a **sensitivity section** re-derives the
   headline numbers across cost ratios 4:1/8:1/16:1 and a second temperature
   trace; and the oscillation ablation shows naive > cooldown-only > full
   policy set in switch counts on a boundary-hover input. All decision
   parameters (including the Execution.Limit cooldown and hysteresis and the
   phenomena that the DeclarativeTarget's goal predicate requires) are read
   from the policy XMI files, not from the harness.
   (b) *Traffic (cross-simulator reuse)*: the **unmodified** adaptation manager
   and policy parser interpret structurally identical policy XMI against a
   second, independently implemented simulator (`examples/traffic-scenario/`)
   through a second translation-layer adapter that shares no code with the
   E-site adapter. Expected outcome: exactly two switches on the
   approach–follow–recede input, cheaper than always-micro at zero relevance
   error, stable on a boundary-hover input, and the import-identity check
   confirming the decision layer is reused byte-for-byte.
5. **Randomized differential validation** (paper: paragraph "Randomized
   differential validation"): 500 seeded random threshold-structured policy
   sets are executed with the unmodified adaptation manager on adversarial
   boundary-hover input batteries; the observed runtime behavior (stable /
   rate-bounded / fast alternation) is checked against the combined verdict
   of validator and static analyses. Expected outcome: no fast-alternating
   set escapes both validator and analyzer (E1); no alternating set goes
   entirely unreported (E2); all three behavior classes occur (E3); and the
   pre-refinement analyzer's misses are reproduced (E4) -- this study
   discovered the pairwise-handover blind spot (now analysis A4), the
   unsound all-temporal downgrade of the rotation analysis A3, and the
   rate-bounded (rather than safe) nature of Limit/TemporalCondition
   mitigation at a shared boundary (now an explicit A2 NOTE).

## Contents

| Path | Description |
| --- | --- |
| `switchingpolicy.ecore` | The metamodel as an EMF/Ecore model (normative structure): `SwitchingPolicy` with six aspects (`Condition`, `Evaluation`, `Target`, `Execution`, `Scope`, `Priority`), context layers, enumerations. |
| `switchingpolicy.ocl` | The well-formedness constraints (WF1, WF2, WF3a, WF3b, WF4, WF5) as OCL invariants (normative constraints), plus three structural invariants without a WF number (see below). |
| `tools/policy_model.py` | XMI parser / in-memory model for policy instances (stdlib only). |
| `tools/validator.py` | Executable reference implementation of WF1–WF5 (with WF3 split into WF3a/WF3b), plus the structural invariants `OperandArity`, `RangeBounds`, `SwitchWindowNeedsBudget` (`check_structure`, reported separately, not part of the WF table). Also usable standalone: `python3 tools/validator.py <file-or-dir>`. |
| `tools/analyzer.py` | Static policy-set analysis: A1 unresolved conflicts, A2 ping-pong/oscillation risks (antagonistic thresholds; rate-bounded mitigation reported as NOTE), A3 n-policy rotation risks (jointly satisfiable activation regions across >= 3 imperative targets), and A4 pairwise handover risks (the n = 2 rotation case, which declared Priorities do not prevent). Standalone: `python3 tools/analyzer.py <policyset-dir>`. |
| `tools/comparative.py` | Derives the comparative-expressiveness verdicts from `coding/*.csv` with a fixed monotone rule (full/partial/no). |
| `tools/adaptation_manager.py` | Reference adaptation manager: interprets policy instances (conditions incl. IN_RANGE, NOT, and the temporal operators HELD_FOR/WITHIN_WINDOW/SINCE_EVENT at any nesting depth; phase gating; the scope's temporal extent; prerequisites; declarative-target reasoning from the goal predicate over validity frames; per-policy limits: cooldown, switch budget incl. ONCE and `switchWindow`, hysteresis re-arm) and arbitrates policy sets by priority (ties by policy name, counted as conflicts). Semantics: section *Runtime semantics* below. |
| `tools/test_runtime_semantics.py` | Unit tests of these runtime semantics, the parser additions, the structural checks, and the analyzer's handling of IN_RANGE/NOT. Not part of `run_evaluation.py`; run `python3 -m unittest discover -s tools -p "test_*.py"`. |
| `tools/randomized_study.py` | Randomized differential validation of the static analyses against runtime ground truth (Study 5; deterministic, seed recorded in `results/randomized_metrics.json`). |
| `tools/run_evaluation.py` | One-command driver + regression self-test; writes `results/`. |
| `examples/esite-scenario/` | Executable two-level E-site simulation (`esite_simulation.py`), its policy sets (`policies/` well-formed, `policies-naive/` deliberately ill-formed), and the scenario driver `run_scenario.py` (incl. sensitivity analysis). |
| `examples/traffic-scenario/` | Second executable two-level simulation (`traffic_simulation.py`, an independent translation-layer adapter in the traffic domain), its well-formed policy set (`policies/`), and the reuse driver `run_traffic.py`. |
| `examples/sumo-binding/` | OPTIONAL third-party binding (microscopic traffic): TraCI-based translation layer (`sumo_adapter.py`) plus scenario files and driver (`run_sumo.py`) that drive SUMO with the SAME policy XMI files as the toy traffic scenario. Requires SUMO (`pip install eclipse-sumo traci sumolib`); reported as SKIPPED otherwise. `--mock` = wiring test only. |
| `examples/mesa-binding/` | OPTIONAL third-party binding (agent-based paradigm): translation layer (`mesa_adapter.py`) + driver (`run_mesa.py`) that drive a Mesa ABM, realizing the View/Zoom multi-level patterns (per-agent level-of-detail by distance to a focus). Policies structurally identical to the traffic scenario's. Requires Mesa (`pip install mesa`); SKIPPED otherwise. `--mock` = wiring test only. |
| `run_thirdparty_studies.ps1`, `run_sumo_study.ps1` | Windows helpers: create an isolated venv next to the script, install SUMO+Mesa (or SUMO only) into it, run the third-party stud(ies) and then the full suite. In the project repository they live in `../env/`; in this archive they sit at the root and auto-detect the layout. |
| `examples/valid/` | Well-formed policy instances: E-site battery switch, Weber et al. time-boundary switch, Huang & Vittal held-out mode switch. |
| `examples/invalid/` | Six seeded-violation instances (one per WF constraint variant) + `expected.json` (expected detections). |
| `examples/policysets/` | `ping-pong-conflict/` + `hysteresis-safe/` (pairwise A1/A2 cases) and `three-cycle-conflict/` + `three-cycle-safe/` (n-policy rotation cases for A3). |
| `static-domains.md` | Per-domain account of static (between-run) level selection, supplementing the synthesized static forms in Section 5.1 of the paper. |
| `dynamic-domains.md` | Per-domain account of dynamic (within-run) switching, supplementing the synthesized dynamic forms in Section 5.1 of the paper. |
| `recurrence.md` | Full pattern-recurrence grouping (five patterns × independent domains × shared policy shape), summarized in the paper's coverage-mapping section. |
| `heldout_zehe.md` | Full aspect-by-aspect mapping of the second held-out case (zehe2015traffic), summarized in the paper. |
| `mapping_weber2024.md` | Full aspect-by-aspect version of the two Weber2024 scenario mappings (time-based and component-based switch), summarized in the paper. |
| `condition_examples.md` | Condition-type examples (Threshold/Relation) per context layer, summarized in the paper's Condition subsection. |
| `reimplementation_availability.md` | Full availability assessment of the surveyed dynamic approaches (why external reimplementation was infeasible), summarized in the paper's executable-instantiation section. |
| `coding/coverage.csv` | Coding sheet of the coverage analysis: 13 cases × 6 aspects, coded Explicit/Implicit/absent, with per-case rationale. |
| `coding/requirements.csv` | Per case: the features it explicitly requires (derived from the explicit codings), with rationale. |
| `coding/capabilities.csv` | Per formalism (ECA, Stitch, FCC, SPM) and feature: full/partial/none, with a written rationale per cell. **To challenge a verdict in the paper, edit the relevant cell and re-run.** |
| `results/` | Generated reports (`validation.md`, `analysis.md`, `comparative.md`, `comparative_sensitivity.md`, `scenario.md`, `traffic.md`, `randomized.md`) and LaTeX rows for the paper tables. |

## Relation between OCL and Python

`switchingpolicy.ocl` is the normative formulation of the constraints;
`tools/validator.py` is an executable reference implementation of the same
invariants (correspondence: OCL `ObservablesReachableFromScope` ↔ WF1,
`GoalPredicatePresent`/`UtilityFunctionPresent` ↔ WF2, `PositiveWindow`/
`AtLeastTwoProperties` ↔ WF3a/WF3b, `OscillationSafety` ↔ WF4, `RankConsistency` ↔ WF5).
The OCL file additionally states three structural invariants without a WF
number, `OperandArity` (AND/OR ≥ 2 operands, NOT exactly 1), `RangeBounds`
(`upperBound` set exactly for IN_RANGE, and not below `bound`), and
`SwitchWindowNeedsBudget` (`switchWindow` needs `maxSwitches` and is > 0);
`validator.check_structure` implements them and keeps them out of the WF
validation table.
The Python implementation exists so that the package runs anywhere without an
Eclipse/EMF installation; users with EMF tooling can equivalently load the
`.ecore` + `.ocl` pair and validate the XMI instances there.

## Runtime semantics of the reference adaptation manager

`tools/adaptation_manager.py` fixes one executable reading of the policy
aspects; it is the reading the executable studies use.

* **Evaluation points.** A call of `propose()` at which the policy's PhaseKind
  is admissible, `now` lies in the closed interval of `Scope.temporalExtent`
  (a missing bound is unbounded), and all evaluation prerequisites hold is an
  evaluation point. Only there is the condition evaluated and can a policy be
  re-armed.
* **Conditions.** `IN_RANGE` reads the closed band `[bound, upperBound]`; NOT
  negates its single operand; all operands are always evaluated. Temporal
  operators are read over the samples at evaluation points, with state per
  structurally equal TemporalCondition (also when nested; see *Shared temporal
  state*), window w (0 if absent):
  `HELD_FOR` holds once its inner condition has been true at every sample for
  at least w; `WITHIN_WINDOW` holds if the inner condition was true at a sample
  at most w ago (so the inner condition implies it); `SINCE_EVENT` holds once
  at least w has passed since the last event, an event being a sample at which
  the inner condition is true and was false at the previous sample (or no
  previous sample exists).
* **Shared temporal state.** Temporal conditions that are structurally equal
  (operator, window, and inner tree incl. observables, operators, and bounds)
  share one sample state, and within a `PolicySetManager` all policies share
  it: equal temporal conditions of several policies are one variable, as in
  the T01 encoding (one auxiliary variable), updated from the union of the
  samples any of these policies takes. Policies with different evaluation
  points (phase, temporal extent, evaluation prerequisites) therefore read the
  same value whenever they evaluate at the same time. Sampling a node again in
  the same round (one `step()` at one `now`) returns its first value and
  updates nothing, so SINCE_EVENT sees each edge once. A stand-alone
  `AdaptationManager` (e.g. one per agent in the Mesa binding) keeps its own
  state.
* **Targets.** An ImperativeTarget is applied directly. A DeclarativeTarget's
  goal predicate is split at ` and ` into the clauses
  `model.validityFrame covers currentOperatingPoint` and
  `model.resolves(p1, ...)`; a bare comma list of phenomena is short for both
  clauses. A predicate with any other clause is still accepted by the parser,
  because the metamodel leaves the semantics of goal predicates to the
  reasoning component (the reason is kept in `Policy.goal_error`); the default
  reasoner raises `ValueError` when it has to evaluate such a predicate, and an
  empty predicate remains a WF2 violation. The default
  reasoner picks the cheapest library model satisfying all clauses (ties: first
  in library order). It does not prefer the current model: if a cheaper model
  also qualifies, the policy proposes a switch even when the current model is
  valid. A UtilityTarget's function is not interpreted; the reasoner then
  picks the cheapest model that covers the operating point and resolves the
  phenomena an adapter may list under `required_phenomena`.
* **Execution.** After a true condition and a target different from the
  active model, a switch is blocked, in this order, by the policy's cooldown
  (time since its *own* last switch), by its switch budget (`maxSwitches`;
  pattern ONCE caps it at 1; counted over the whole run, or over the last
  `switchWindow` time units), and, with `hysteresis`, until it is re-armed. A
  policy starts armed, its own switch disarms it, and every evaluation point
  with a false condition re-arms it, regardless of cooldown, budget,
  satisfaction, or precedence. Execution prerequisites are checked last.
* **Priority.** `PolicySetManager` lets the policy with the lowest explicit
  rank switch (unranked policies yield to ranked ones). Among proposals of
  equal minimal rank the policy with the smallest name wins, and if they want
  different targets the round is counted in `conflicts` and logged with time
  and policy names in `conflict_log`.

## Scope

The metamodel is intentionally confined to the *decision* layer. Model
conversion, state transfer, and consistency maintenance are out of scope by
design and are expected to be provided by a complementary conversion layer and
simulator-specific adapters (the *Translation Layer* in the paper).

The reference adaptation manager and the E-site scenario are intentionally
minimal: they demonstrate that policies specified in the metamodel are
operational and measure the decision-layer overhead, not the performance of a
production simulator. Integrating the manager into a full-scale simulator is
the natural next step; the `SimulationAdapter` protocol in
`tools/adaptation_manager.py` is the single surface such an integration must
implement.
